# Auto-Organize configuration
# Get a key at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey — never commit your real key.
# Optional: without a key, Auto-Organize still works from deterministic
# domain/keyword clustering alone — this key only sharpens it with
# semantic similarity hints.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Optional override (a sane default is baked in if omitted). Verify against
# https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/models for your account before
# relying on the default — model availability/pricing changes.
#
# GEMINI_EMBEDDING_MODEL — embeds tab content for Auto-Organize's semantic
# clustering hints.
# GEMINI_EMBEDDING_MODEL=gemini-embedding-001
#
# GEMINI_GENERATION_MODEL — batch-level hierarchical section organization
# (Category -> Subcategory -> Project), see src/lib/sections/ai/organize.ts.
# Falls back to deterministic category/cluster-based organization when unset
# or unreachable — a dump never fails because of this.
# GEMINI_GENERATION_MODEL=gemini-flash-lite-latest

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Accounts (Sign in with Google)
#
# Entirely optional: with none of these set, TabDump runs exactly as it did
# before accounts existed — no sign-in UI, no auth requests, workspaces in
# this browser's local storage as always.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The OAuth 2.0 Web application client ID from Google Cloud Console
# (APIs & Services -> Credentials). Safe to expose: the browser needs it to
# start a sign-in, and a token minted with it still has to pass server-side
# verification here before it means anything.
#
# One variable, not two, on purpose — the server checks incoming ID tokens
# against exactly the client ID the browser signed in with, so they cannot
# drift apart. (GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID is accepted as a server-side alias if you
# already set it out of habit; NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID wins.)
#
# Add these under "Authorized JavaScript origins" for that client:
#   http://localhost:3000        (local development)
#   https://tabsdump.vercel.app  (production)
# No redirect URI is needed — this flow never leaves your site.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# There is deliberately NO GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET here. TabDump verifies
# Google ID tokens against Google's published public keys, which needs no
# secret; nothing in this codebase reads one. If you have one, leave it out
# of this project's environment entirely.

# Postgres connection string for the accounts/sessions tables. Required for
# sign-in to work in production: sessions are server-side rows (see
# src/lib/auth/store/schema.sql), and a serverless deployment has nowhere
# else to keep them. Vercel Postgres injects POSTGRES_URL for you; any
# Postgres works. Apply the schema once with `npm run migrate:auth`.
#
# Local development without this falls back to an in-memory store, so you
# can sign in and out without provisioning anything — sessions just don't
# survive a dev-server restart.
# POSTGRES_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Desktop app (Tauri)
#
# Optional, and unset for the desktop build this repo produces today.
#
# A packaged desktop build is served from tauri://localhost, where a relative
# "/api/..." resolves to the bundle itself rather than to a server. Setting
# this points those calls at a deployed TabDump instead.
#
# Leave it unset unless you have first added an explicit
# Access-Control-Allow-Origin allowlist entry for the desktop origin on the
# unauthenticated routes (/api/titles, /api/ai/*) — without CORS headers the
# webview blocks the request regardless of this value. It does NOT enable
# desktop sign-in: the session cookie is HttpOnly + SameSite=Lax and
# same-origin, so accounts need the pairing flow described in
# docs/desktop-architecture.md, not a relaxed cookie.
#
# Nothing breaks while it is unset: title resolution falls back to domain
# names and Auto-Organize falls back to deterministic clustering, exactly as
# they already do when offline.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TABDUMP_API_ORIGIN=https://tabsdump.vercel.app

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Provider connections (bring your own credentials)
#
# Each TabDump user connects their OWN AI provider credentials, through
# Settings -> AI Connectors. TabDump does not ship a shared Claude account and
# does not run anybody's agent on a credential that is not theirs.
#
# The key below encrypts those credentials at rest (AES-256-GCM). It is
# REQUIRED: without it TabDump refuses to store or read a provider credential
# at all, so nobody can connect and no session can start. That is deliberate —
# a deployment that cannot encrypt a credential has no business holding one,
# and falling back to plaintext would be an invented guarantee.
#
# Generate one with:
#
#   node scripts/migrate-credentials.mjs --key
#
# Then create the tables with:
#
#   npm run migrate:credentials
#
# Rotating or losing this key makes every stored credential unreadable and
# every user reconnects. Back it up where you back up your database password,
# and NOT beside the database.
# TABDUMP_CREDENTIAL_KEY=

# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is deliberately absent from this file.
#
# It used to be the deployment-wide operator key that ran every user's agent,
# which meant the deployment's owner paid for everybody's runs. Phase I.2
# removed that: nothing reads it to start a session any more. A key left in
# the environment is inert — the local runtime strips it out of the agent
# process's environment, and the remote runtime never reads it.
#
# The one exception is the opt-in integration suite, where it is a developer's
# own key for a developer's own test. See docs/provider-connections.md.
